The F0 FAIL follow-up: does NSApp.mainMenu regain authority once the
SwiftUI App/Scene lifecycle is absent entirely?

- F0PrimeShell.swift: the App struct's state graph + wiring task body +
  helpers transplanted verbatim (mechanical perl transforms only) into
  F0PrimeShellFace — a View STRUCT with same-named properties (value
  semantics keep every extracted closure's implicit-self capture
  identical to production). The value-type @State (six fields) + the
  per-session rebuilt optionals move into F0PrimeShellGraph (@Observable
  box): @State storage is not shared across struct copies, and the NSMenu
  closures + shell delegate write from OUTSIDE the hosted view tree.
  F0PrimeShellController owns the objects; F0PrimeShellDelegate composes
  the production AppDelegate (final — open AppleEvents + willTerminate
  flush forwarded verbatim); the entry point swaps LightamerApp.main()
  for F0PrimeShellMain.run() (manual NSApplication + regular policy +
  NSWindow 1440x900 + NSHostingView).
- NSMenuBridge.swift: the F0 bridge tree (flag enum dropped — install is
  unconditional; this whole build IS the experiment arm). Window/Help
  menus registered on NSApp; MenuCommandSet consumed via the same
  visibleCommandIDs; workspace/metadata-focus/export-presence noted
  through the F0 publish-site hunks (WorkspaceHostView/InspectorView/
  ContentView — reused verbatim).
- LightamerApp.swift: WindowCapture private->internal (the extracted
  face reuses it); the probe-stay hunk from commit 1 rides along.

Spike-only: never merges to main.
